'''3.''' ''pl.'' The remains of the human body when burned; hence, a dead body or corpse; mortal remains.
{{smaller block|<poem>Poor key-cold figure of a holy king!
Pale ''ashes'' of the house of Lancaster!
Thou bloodless remnant of that royal blood!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., i. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Black ashes''', crude soda.—'''Blue ashes.''' See ''blue''.—'''Clavellated ashes.''' See ''clavellated''.—'''Dust and ashes''', a Scriptural phrase expressive, when applied to one’s self, of deep humiliation: as, “I which am but ''dust and ashes'',” Gen. xviii. 27.—'''Ultramarine ashes.''' See ''ultramarine''.}}

'''ash'''<sup>2</sup> (ash), ''v. t.'' [< ''ash''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' To strew or sprinkle with ashes.
{{smaller block|They ''ash'' and powder their pericraniums.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, iv. 5.}}}}
'''2.''' To convert into ashes.
{{smaller block|The folded filter paper brought into a scorifier and ''ashed'' in a glowing muffle. {{right|''Amer. Chem. Jour.'', VIII. 78.}}}}

'''ashame''' (a-shām′), ''v.'' [< (1) ME. ''aschamen'', ''ashamen'', < AS. ''āscamian'', ''āsceamian'' (= MHG. ''irscamen'', ''erschemen'', G. ''erschämen''); mixed with (2) ME. ''yshamen'', ''yschamen'', < AS. ''gescamian'', ''gesceamian'', ''gescomian'' (= Goth. ''gaskaman'', refl.), and (3) ME. ''ofschamen'', < AS. ''*ofscamian'' (the last two in ME. only in pp.); < AS. ''ā-'' (E. ''a-''<sup>1</sup>), AS. ''ge-'' (E. ''a-''<sup>6</sup>), or AS. ''of-'' (E. ''a-''<sup>4</sup>), respectively, + ''scamian'', ''sceamian'', shame: see ''a-''<sup>1</sup>, ''a-''<sup>6</sup>, ''a-''<sup>4</sup>, and ''shame'', ''v.''] '''I.†''' ''intrans.'' To feel shame; be

readings.c1s2:
ashamed.

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To shame; make ashamed. [Now rarely used except in the past participle ''ashamed'', with the force of an adjective.]
{{smaller block|It should humble, ''ashame'' and grieve us.
{{right|''Barrow'', Works, II. 417.}}}}

'''ashamed''' (a-shāmd′), ''p. a.'' [< (1) ME. ''ashamed'', ''aschamed'', < AS. ''āscamod'', mixed with (2) ME. ''ysshamed'', < AS. ''gescamod'', and (3) ME. ''ofschamed'', < AS. *''ofscamod''; pp. of the preceding verb.] '''1.''' Affected or touched by shame; abashed or confused by guilt or a conviction of some wrong action, indecorous conduct, or other impropriety: hardly used attributively: followed by ''of'', or by a dependent clause with ''that''.
{{smaller block|They shall be turned back, they shall be greatly ''ashamed'', that trust in graven images.
{{right|Is. xlii. 17.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I feel sufficiently my folly’s penance,
And am ''asham’d''; that shame a thousand sorrows
Feed on continually. ''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, v. 7.</poem>}}
{{smaller block|Those who base their hopes for the future on the glorious revelations of the Bible need not be ''ashamed of'' its story of the past. ''Dawson'', Nature and the Bible, p. 181.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>“Thy name?” . . .
“''Ashamed'' am I ''that'' I should tell it thee.
My pride is broken: men have seen my fall.”</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
'''2.''' Reluctant through fear of shame: followed by an infinitive: as, I am ''ashamed'' to offer it, it is so little.
{{smaller block|I cannot dig, to beg I am ''ashamed''.
{{right|Luke xvi. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|He was not ''ashamed'' to answer that he could not live out of the royal smile.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng.}}}}

